          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a urinary tract infection, and why are they more common in women?",
          "answer": "A urinary tract infection (UTI) is a bacterial infection of the urinary system. Most involve the bladder and urethra, the tube that carries urine out of the body. This is called a lower tract infection, or cystitis. Infection that reaches the kidney is a more serious illness called pyelonephritis. The main cause is *Escherichia coli* bacteria. (Source: General clinical reference, Urinary tract infection.)\n\nThe retrieved passages list female anatomy, with a shorter urethra, as one reason UTIs are more common in women. They also name sexual activity, certain contraceptives (including spermicides and diaphragms), and changes in vaginal bacteria after menopause. Incomplete bladder emptying, kidney stones, diabetes, and previous infections also raise the risk. (Source: General clinical reference, Urinary tract infection.) In men, infections are less common and are more often linked to an enlarged prostate. (Same source.)\n\nI could not confirm the point about the urethra's closeness to the anus. My last search failed, and the passages I did retrieve do not cover it. A clinician or a trusted medical website can explain that part.\n\nIf someone with a suspected UTI becomes confused, very drowsy, breathless, feverish or shivery, or stops passing urine for many hours, they should seek emergency care straight away. These can be signs of sepsis, a serious response to infection. (Source: Critical care and emergency medicine society guidance, Sepsis.)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
